SE Radio is brought to you by the IEEE Computer Society and by IEEE Software Magazine, online at computer.org/software.

3
00:00:20,750 --> 00:00:22,710
Welcome to Software Engineering Radio. I'm your host Brijesh Ammanath.

4
00:00:22,710 --> 00:00:29,190
Today, we will be discussing privacy and security of AI coding assistants with our guest Luke Hinds.

5
00:00:29,190 --> 00:00:31,390
Luke is the CTO of Stack Lock.

6
00:00:31,390 --> 00:00:33,430
He created Sixstore while a distinguished engineer at Red Hat.

7
00:00:33,430 --> 00:00:42,990
He's a security thought leader, engineer, public speaker who loves building open-source software and communities, as well as leading talented engineering teams to develop innovative,

8
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:47,150
cutting-edge security technologies at scale.

9
00:00:47,150 --> 00:00:49,550
Luke, is there anything I missed that you would like to add to your introduction?

10
00:00:49,560 --> 00:00:54,230
No, you pretty much covered it all there. Yeah, and in a very succinct and on point.

11
00:00:54,240 --> 00:00:56,590
Thanks.

12
00:00:56,590 --> 00:01:01,710
Luke, if you can start off by explaining the increased role of AI coding agents in improving program productivity.

13
00:01:01,720 --> 00:01:05,390
Certainly.

14
00:01:05,390 --> 00:01:12,230
So, this really harks back to 2017 when the paper came out of Google around the Transformers architecture.

15
00:01:12,230 --> 00:01:15,750
The paper was called Attention is all that you need.

16
00:01:15,750 --> 00:01:17,990
It's speaking about the attention mechanism.

17
00:01:17,990 --> 00:01:30,550
Prior to then, we'd had AI machine learning have been around for quite some time, but we hadn't really seen any killer applications of it beyond statistical analysis, and people would use it as a business tool essentially.

18
00:01:30,550 --> 00:01:34,230
And so, we started to see these sort of general-purpose models come out.

19
00:01:34,230 --> 00:01:36,710
And then quite early on, there was of course GitHub Copilot.

20
00:01:36,710 --> 00:01:42,670
And this is where it was quite astonishing really.

21
00:01:42,670 --> 00:01:46,030
Even though the quality was not quite there, the ability for these machines to generate code,

22
00:01:46,030 --> 00:01:54,990
it was noticed as being a very clear emerging use case application of these models was how good they were at generating code.

23
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:59,550
And initially, we started off with a system called fill in the middle.

24
00:01:59,550 --> 00:02:04,550
And fill in the middle is auto completion of code.

25
00:02:04,550 --> 00:02:10,870
And what happens is the code is generated based on the prepend and the append.

26
00:02:10,870 --> 00:02:17,150
So, the content that surrounds the prompt, or effectively where the cursor is situated within a IDE.

27
00:02:17,150 --> 00:02:25,550
Okay, so the prompt would fill up the code that's above, say 10 lines, and the 10 lines below the cursor.

28
00:02:25,550 --> 00:02:29,350
And then the model would have to predict what is in the middle.

29
00:02:29,350 --> 00:02:31,870
So, it was called fill in the middle.

30
00:02:31,870 --> 00:02:33,510
FIM is the term that they use.

31
00:02:33,510 --> 00:02:40,630
So, I think this was one of the first times that we started to see AI really start to get some traction with software engineers.

32
00:02:40,630 --> 00:02:45,470
And you got this auto complete magical to complete functionality that came around.

33
00:02:45,470 --> 00:02:53,190
And that was really where there would have definitely been a marked increase in productivity for people that were using that because as a software engineer,

34
00:02:53,200 --> 00:03:02,590
there would be a lot of tasks that you would perform where you wouldn't really need to exercise any level of really pushing your brain to the limits.

35
00:03:02,590 --> 00:03:12,190
There would be things such as hashing out structs and setting up functions, and just sort of general housekeeping type tasks.

36
00:03:12,190 --> 00:03:14,030
And AI proved to be very good at doing that.

37
00:03:14,030 --> 00:03:16,509
I remember when I started to use Copilot myself,

38
00:03:16,509 --> 00:03:28,910
I was quite surprised at how it would understand precisely the response format that I would need to set out for a struct or it would somehow magically understand what I needed for a JSON structure.

39
00:03:28,910 --> 00:03:37,470
And this is where we really started to see, I think, a productivity increase was In a lot ways, it was a tool as a developer tool,

40
00:03:37,470 --> 00:03:45,390
in much the same way as when shells started to become more purposed to developers, and you started to get that sort of rich experience within a terminal.

41
00:03:45,390 --> 00:03:49,750
And people made them very much their own thing because it improved their productivity essentially.

42
00:03:49,750 --> 00:03:56,670
And that was the start of the productivity gain that has happened within software development.

43
00:03:56,670 --> 00:04:04,750
I think there is a lot more future applications of AI that will increase productivity and will help a lot to reduce churn.

44
00:04:04,750 --> 00:04:10,550
It will take away the grind of a lot of stuff that engineers have had to do, they've had to live with and accept.

45
00:04:10,550 --> 00:04:14,670
I think we'll start to see AI factor those way over time as well.

46
00:04:14,680 --> 00:04:16,949
All right.

47
00:04:16,949 --> 00:04:19,630
You've covered GitHub Copilot in one of our previous episodes, 533.

48
00:04:19,640 --> 00:04:21,910
I'll make sure we'll link it to that.

49
00:04:21,920 --> 00:04:27,710
Have any studies been done to quantify the improved productivity by using AI coding assistance?

50
00:04:27,720 --> 00:04:30,430
Yes, there are.

51
00:04:30,430 --> 00:04:40,550
Now, people have different views as to how accurate they are, but one of the ones that I heard was it was related to GitHub and it was around the region of 45 to 50%.

52
00:04:40,550 --> 00:04:49,110
I cannot remember the exact figure now, but there's quite a substantial claim that productivity had been significantly raised through the use of AI.

53
00:04:49,120 --> 00:04:50,870
That's quite a large number.

54
00:04:50,880 --> 00:04:52,870
Certainly it certainly is.

55
00:04:52,870 --> 00:05:00,230
And we have noticed a marked ability to be able to move more quickly with what we build, thank you to AI.

56
00:05:00,230 --> 00:05:06,630
So, I'm part of a startup and we use AI very effectively for prototyping.

57
00:05:06,630 --> 00:05:09,310
Okay, so we wouldn't really trust it in a large code base.

58
00:05:09,310 --> 00:05:17,830
It just doesn't handle large code bases so well, but when you very quickly want to prototype and build something, that's where AI is exceptionally strong.

59
00:05:17,830 --> 00:05:26,710
To move from zero to something you can act very quickly and that's very useful in a fast-moving environment such as a startup

60
00:05:26,710 --> 00:05:35,590
where you need to quickly validate ideas, you need to connect up and integrate different systems, and AI can be great there cuz you just want to see something wiggle.

61
00:05:35,590 --> 00:05:37,430
You want to see something working.

62
00:05:37,430 --> 00:05:40,230
You want to prove the hypothesis to yourself.

63
00:05:40,230 --> 00:05:52,190
And that's where I found I I've really embraced AI myself is to do a lot of tasks where I need to move fast, I need to be reactive, I want to try something out, I want to validate something.

64
00:05:52,190 --> 00:05:54,070
That is where AI's really come into its own.

65
00:05:54,070 --> 00:05:58,390
For myself and some of the engineers that I have in my company working on producing software.

66
00:05:58,400 --> 00:06:03,830
And what are the key risks introduced due to the reliance on AI coding assistance?

67
00:06:03,840 --> 00:06:06,910
So, there are many.

68
00:06:06,910 --> 00:06:10,510
Okay, some of them are old risks within the new world.

69
00:06:10,510 --> 00:06:17,150
We looked at coding assistance and did some studies on their security.

70
00:06:17,150 --> 00:06:20,750
What are the risks?

71
00:06:20,750 --> 00:06:29,270
And I think one of the clear ones that would became immediately present was data exfiltration, even if unwillingly.

72
00:06:29,270 --> 00:06:33,030
The leakage of secrets, tokens, passwords, personal information.

73
00:06:33,040 --> 00:06:37,790
Essentially, coding assistants, they rely on context.

74
00:06:37,790 --> 00:06:40,790
Context is really important.

75
00:06:40,790 --> 00:06:42,790
So, context is essentially snippets of code.

76
00:06:42,790 --> 00:06:50,390
They need to understand the entire or at least a section of a syntax tree within code,

77
00:06:50,390 --> 00:06:57,190
so that they can make changes without breaking other functions or methods that belong to a particular class that they're working within.

78
00:06:57,190 --> 00:06:59,510
So, they like to grab entire files.

79
00:06:59,510 --> 00:07:08,230
That's one of the things you'll see quite often when you're working with a coding assistant such as Cursor or Copilot, they will take a whole file via the IDE,

80
00:07:08,240 --> 00:07:23,030
they'll load it into a prompt, and then that prompt will then be sent to an endpoint where it'll be canonicalized and serialized and put into a format that the LLM can then make a prediction around what the task is at hand.

81
00:07:23,030 --> 00:07:25,710
It'll start to generate code.

82
00:07:25,710 --> 00:07:31,030
And quite often, these files can be it could be cryptographic keys,

83
00:07:31,030 --> 00:07:39,071
they could be.env files where people have database passwords and perhaps for a production site. It could be API tokens.

84
00:07:39,071 --> 00:07:45,790
Quite often, I spoke about earlier being able to prototype very quickly, that's one of the useful things for AI.

85
00:07:45,790 --> 00:07:47,550
We've all done it as software engineers.

86
00:07:47,550 --> 00:07:50,710
You are writing a single script.

87
00:07:50,710 --> 00:07:53,310
You want to see an API work.

88
00:07:53,310 --> 00:07:56,470
You hardcode the token in because you know you're not going to push it to GitHub.

89
00:07:56,470 --> 00:07:59,670
You're certainly not going to deploy it, but you just want to see the thing work.

90
00:07:59,670 --> 00:08:01,390
So, you take a token and you hardcode it.

91
00:08:01,390 --> 00:08:06,910
And those effectively are captured by the IDE and they're all sent out.

92
00:08:06,910 --> 00:08:11,830
And these keys are I mean, it's you could discuss the receiving party could be trusted.

93
00:08:11,830 --> 00:08:20,070
Could be a large corporation, Microsoft Azure or Anthropic or wherever the cloud service is running.

94
00:08:20,070 --> 00:08:26,909
They may well be entities that you should never leak keys, but they're not likely to exploit you by receiving those keys.

95
00:08:26,909 --> 00:08:34,350
But I think the other concern is that quite often the data that is received is used to train models.

96
00:08:34,350 --> 00:08:43,589
So, hypothetically, it's not too far-fetched to have a scenario where a large language model may spit out a token that it's learned during its training phase.

97
00:08:43,589 --> 00:08:45,790
It could spit out a password.

98
00:08:45,790 --> 00:08:48,030
It could spit out a bit of personal information.

99
00:08:48,030 --> 00:08:53,790
You may have a spreadsheet that you by accident put within a folder that contains code and

100
00:08:53,800 --> 00:09:03,510
that could be picked up by the IDE and it could have payslip information, social security numbers, medical information.

101
00:09:03,510 --> 00:09:05,430
And this data can then be used for reinforcement learning.

102
00:09:05,430 --> 00:09:09,430
It can be used to train the model because data is king effectively.

103
00:09:09,430 --> 00:09:13,110
Data is the key value that is derived from a lot of these services.

104
00:09:13,110 --> 00:09:18,270
So, those are the key risks I think for developers is that it's very easy to leak information.

105
00:09:18,270 --> 00:09:26,230
For the actual models themselves, I think some of the other risks are quite often they have a distant training dates.

106
00:09:26,240 --> 00:09:33,829
Okay, so knowledge is a model is tuned and the knowledge is recent at the point of where that training happens.

107
00:09:33,839 --> 00:09:40,230
Right. Now, afterwards, knowledge becomes stale effectively.

108
00:09:40,230 --> 00:09:42,271
So, it's at a certain point in time. It's a snapshot in time.

109
00:09:42,271 --> 00:09:48,350
And because these models are very expensive to do full training, it's not something that happens every week or month, even.

110
00:09:48,360 --> 00:09:52,550
Sometimes it can be months that will go by.

111
00:09:52,550 --> 00:09:57,030
A model will be in circulation, and then the next new frontier model will come out.

112
00:09:57,030 --> 00:10:00,111
And during that time, security risks can happen. Okay?

113
00:10:00,111 --> 00:10:08,790
Packages can become malicious, maintainer accounts can be overtaken, vulnerabilities can be discovered, critical vulnerabilities.

114
00:10:08,800 --> 00:10:14,230
And the large language model will have no implicit knowledge of that because of its training cutoff date.

115
00:10:14,230 --> 00:10:19,150
So, there is a chance that a model could recommend dangerous content, effectively.

116
00:10:19,150 --> 00:10:26,350
You know, it could introduce security risks into your code because it doesn't have that current knowledge that it is a risk.

117
00:10:26,350 --> 00:10:28,711
I guess one way of highlighting this is we remember Log4j. Okay?

118
00:10:28,711 --> 00:10:38,150
This is sort of way before the current batch of large language models and the transformer architecture was in public use.

119
00:10:38,150 --> 00:10:44,990
But you imagine a model had been trained, say a week before Log4j, and then the next new model would not come out for 4 or 5 months later.

120
00:10:44,990 --> 00:10:54,910
It could quite easily be creating people's dependency files for when they want to create a Java project, and then in there is Log4j, effectively.

121
00:10:54,910 --> 00:11:01,550
So, yeah, I'd say stale knowledge cutoffs are one of the things that we look to do in Stack Clock was

122
00:11:01,550 --> 00:11:12,550
that we use an embeddings model and a retrieval-augmented generation system to bring in the latest threat intelligence around what's dangerous, malicious, archived, deprecated, and so forth.

123
00:11:12,550 --> 00:11:16,670
So, there are ways that you can get more fresh, recent information in there.

124
00:11:16,670 --> 00:11:18,270
The other, as I said, is is leaking.

125
00:11:18,270 --> 00:11:22,829
That's something else that we've also looked to address on the tail end of our research.

126
00:11:22,829 --> 00:11:25,790
And in general, just hallucinations.

127
00:11:25,790 --> 00:11:29,470
Models will come out with functions that do not exist.

128
00:11:29,470 --> 00:11:36,630
It will be sketching out some code that uses an upstream library, a dependency, and it will just make up names.

129
00:11:36,630 --> 00:11:39,830
It will come out with function names that don't exist.

130
00:11:39,830 --> 00:11:50,790
And there is a chance that if that's happening at a high frequency, then somebody could attempt to kind of squat on these hallucinated names and weaponize them.

131
00:11:50,790 --> 00:11:52,310
So, those are some of the key risks.

132
00:11:52,310 --> 00:11:57,270
And I mean, there are other things such as prompt injections and insecure models.

133
00:11:57,270 --> 00:12:06,550
Models have, you know, they use PyTorch and you've got It's not used so much now, but pickle, which was susceptible to various attacks, and they started to use something called safe tensors,

134
00:12:06,550 --> 00:12:14,470
which is a lot more safer now around the execution of code, but I would say those are the key aspects, the key security concerns.

135
00:12:14,480 --> 00:12:20,510
All right, quite a few key points over there, and we will double click on two of them specifically.

136
00:12:20,510 --> 00:12:24,630
One is secrets management, and the other one is dependency injection in this session.

137
00:12:24,640 --> 00:12:31,990
It's a good leeway into the next theme of the podcast, which is around secrets management.

138
00:12:31,990 --> 00:12:40,710
You've elaborated on what kind of secrets could be leaked, but what risk does the passage of secrets to LLMs pose?

139
00:12:40,710 --> 00:12:43,430
Is it primarily that once it's used in training, it could be exposed?

140
00:12:43,440 --> 00:12:49,990
Or are there other risks because of LLMs having that secrets within its context?

141
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:55,470
I guess it would be who receives the secret.

142
00:12:55,470 --> 00:12:59,910
Okay, so there are some organizations, corporations that we're going to feel a level of trust towards.

143
00:12:59,920 --> 00:13:08,990
If they're a large business that has a reputation, why would they want to use an individual's key to exploit a system? It's not very likely to happen.

144
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:21,150
Mhm. So, I would say with these large vendors that have these frontier models, the risk is much likely going to be that the large language model regurgitates a secret or a key that it's discovered.

145
00:13:21,160 --> 00:13:32,110
Now, if you were to use a provider who is less well established, does not have a brand that they're going to be very protective of,

146
00:13:32,110 --> 00:13:42,070
does not have a core business that's financially lucrative enough that they don't need to resort to nefarious malicious actions, then that could be a risk.

147
00:13:42,070 --> 00:13:49,630
I mean, this was one of the things that people, perhaps rightly or wrongly, inferred for Deep Seek when they put up an API.

148
00:13:49,630 --> 00:13:51,390
I have absolutely no opinion myself there.

149
00:13:51,400 --> 00:14:02,350
People were effectively saying, you know, your secrets are being leaked to a company which operates outside of the jurisdiction of where most software engineers and companies tend to operate.

150
00:14:02,360 --> 00:14:06,070
So, I'm actually not insinuating anything at all there myself.

151
00:14:06,070 --> 00:14:11,150
The Deep Seek people seem like very bright, smart people,

152
00:14:11,150 --> 00:14:19,070
but there was this element of if these models are the inference points are much wider, and there are many more providers,

153
00:14:19,070 --> 00:14:23,350
then obviously your secrets are going to be leaked to many more APIs, many more cloud services.

154
00:14:23,350 --> 00:14:27,550
They're going to be stored in many more logs and monitoring systems and so forth.

155
00:14:27,550 --> 00:14:33,829
And so, somebody effectively with bad intentions could possibly exfiltrate those secrets and use them.

156
00:14:33,839 --> 00:14:42,550
And how easy is it for malicious actors to extract secrets or secret data from LLMs?

157
00:14:42,550 --> 00:14:45,950
Is it through prompt engineering or are there other methods they use?

158
00:14:45,960 --> 00:14:48,710
It would be through prompt engineering.

159
00:14:48,720 --> 00:14:51,110
I would say it's not easy.

160
00:14:51,110 --> 00:14:58,870
It would require probably a prompt injection attack to get the large language model to break out of its safeguards.

161
00:14:58,870 --> 00:15:04,670
So, large language models are trained so that they have a set of guardrails around.

162
00:15:04,670 --> 00:15:09,790
There's certain dialogue that they will not enter that consists of physical harm or hate and so forth.

163
00:15:09,790 --> 00:15:11,350
And security is another one of them.

164
00:15:11,350 --> 00:15:21,270
If you ask a lot of large language models, tell me how to write a Bitcoin miner or tell me how to write a script that exploits somebody's computer, it will refuse.

165
00:15:21,270 --> 00:15:23,310
It has its guardrails.

166
00:15:23,310 --> 00:15:25,350
But, they can be broken out of this.

167
00:15:25,350 --> 00:15:32,870
There's a sequence of words that we'll use which will allow them to drop all of that context, that protection.

168
00:15:32,870 --> 00:15:38,310
And that is when you could no doubt likely get them to reveal some of what they've ingested.

169
00:15:38,310 --> 00:15:46,910
I must admit, it's not something I've achieved myself or I've done myself, but prompt injections are not as difficult as you would imagine.

170
00:15:46,910 --> 00:15:53,590
Recently when our Gemini came out, I found one myself that allowed me to break the model completely out of its sort of safe controls.

171
00:15:53,590 --> 00:16:02,670
And that is a concern, effectively, is people break the system outside of its guard rails, and then it could start to reveal some of that information.

172
00:16:02,680 --> 00:16:09,710
Have there been known cases where secrets have been leaked due to AI-generated code? Any examples come to mind?

173
00:16:09,720 --> 00:16:13,990
Have been leaked from the large language model or from a user's environment?

174
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:16,190
Due to use of AI-generated code.

175
00:16:16,200 --> 00:16:25,510
I don't know of a large language model in the wild generating code that uses a secret, that like a cryptographic token.

176
00:16:25,520 --> 00:16:31,350
I imagine there's probably quite a few passwords out there, but it's not something I've seen myself.

177
00:16:31,360 --> 00:16:39,590
Okay, so nothing like the Log4j example, which was before LLMs and before AI-generated code. Nothing major has hit the news.

178
00:16:39,600 --> 00:16:46,470
Not as yet, but I have a feeling something large will arrive.

179
00:16:46,470 --> 00:16:51,670
I don't know of any particular attack that's about to manifest, but I'm pretty sure we'll see one quite soon.

180
00:16:51,680 --> 00:16:54,070
Hopefully not. Fingers crossed. Yes, yeah.

181
00:16:54,080 --> 00:17:02,790
Are there best practices developers can follow when using AI-generated coding assistance beyond just secret scanning and package validation?

182
00:17:02,800 --> 00:17:07,309
Yes.

183
00:17:07,309 --> 00:17:09,829
So, it's very easy to switch off when AI's generating code.

184
00:17:09,829 --> 00:17:19,630
Now, people all have a different propensity to how much they do that, but there's this they call it vibe coding, which is where you essentially let the AI just keep spinning.

185
00:17:19,630 --> 00:17:24,390
You auto approve everything and you allow an AI just have unfettered access to building an application.

186
00:17:24,390 --> 00:17:30,030
I'd say the more tighter that you can review the code that AI generates, the better.

187
00:17:30,040 --> 00:17:39,510
It's much more likely that if you read the code, you scrutinize the code, you're going to notice certain patterns that are insecure,

188
00:17:39,510 --> 00:17:48,670
certain ways that code can be generated where if you really look at it, you'll start to see very simple sort of top 10 OWASP type risks that could be introduced.

189
00:17:48,670 --> 00:17:54,990
Shell execution, unsanitized input is one that I see quite often LLMs will generate.

190
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:58,510
So, always try to sort of stay close to your code.

191
00:17:58,510 --> 00:18:03,430
Try not to switch off too much and let it just have free reign to generate what it wants.

192
00:18:03,430 --> 00:18:07,110
Most senior engineers will will probably be nodding heads going, "Oh, yeah.

193
00:18:07,120 --> 00:18:18,230
Tell me something new." But, something that's very key, I think, as newer engineers start to come in and start to use these tools as as more of a first go-to approach, effectively.

194
00:18:18,230 --> 00:18:19,910
I'd say that's one of the key things.

195
00:18:19,910 --> 00:18:24,310
And then around information leakage awareness, really.

196
00:18:24,310 --> 00:18:30,430
Be aware that anything that's within that folder is likely to be leaked.

197
00:18:30,430 --> 00:18:32,830
So, use environment variables, of course, for secrets.

198
00:18:32,830 --> 00:18:39,830
Or even better, use a proper cryptographic system where you have something like Vault or even a hardware security module or a YubiKey.

199
00:18:39,840 --> 00:18:45,710
Or you start to leverage these technologies so that the secrets are not in the code in the first place.

200
00:18:45,710 --> 00:18:48,910
And then there's also CodeGate, which is something that StackLok we built.

201
00:18:48,910 --> 00:18:53,150
It's an open source project which will provide those protections for you.

202
00:18:53,150 --> 00:18:59,230
You won't even know that you've been at risk of leaking something cuz it will be redacted on the fly.

203
00:18:59,230 --> 00:19:01,990
So, we use lots of technology to to sense secrets leak.

204
00:19:01,990 --> 00:19:07,910
And that's a free to use open source project that anybody can use with their coding assistance.

205
00:19:07,920 --> 00:19:13,670
Why are existing productions like.gitignore or cursor ignore not enough.

206
00:19:13,680 --> 00:19:26,590
I think really because environments I don't know those systems too well, but the truth is that when AI is operating on code I mean code is forever changing.

207
00:19:26,600 --> 00:19:33,830
It's like a get ignore could be good to stop you accidentally checking secrets into your GitHub repository.

208
00:19:33,830 --> 00:19:36,670
You could put a dot M file in there.

209
00:19:36,670 --> 00:19:41,190
You could kind of block these things from being pushed to a repo.

210
00:19:41,190 --> 00:19:47,030
I'm not too aware of the dot cursor rules whether they allow to block secrets or not.

211
00:19:47,030 --> 00:19:52,910
But quite often it's not very deterministic where you're going to put tokens, where you're going to put keys.

212
00:19:52,910 --> 00:19:59,670
You know, if you accidentally get a medical report in your Gmail, you do save as in the browser.

213
00:19:59,670 --> 00:20:07,590
You forgot that the last time you used the save as feature was to download an image that you wanted to use on a website.

214
00:20:07,590 --> 00:20:10,230
And oops, I've saved as my medical report.

215
00:20:10,230 --> 00:20:15,350
It's gone into my code repo folder and now it's being grabbed by the IDE and sent up to the cloud.

216
00:20:15,350 --> 00:20:22,150
I would say things are too arbitrary really to rely on a having to constantly update a text file on your system.

217
00:20:22,160 --> 00:20:24,510
Agreed.

218
00:20:24,510 --> 00:20:31,190
So get ignore or cursor ignore is more about stopping the file being checked in and not from I believe so, yeah.

219
00:20:31,190 --> 00:20:40,150
Like I said, I'm not too aware of the dot cursor what they're doing there, but if it is like get ignore and it stops the IDE from processing the file.

220
00:20:40,150 --> 00:20:42,230
Like I'd say things are a lot more transient really.

221
00:20:42,230 --> 00:20:46,390
They're not as fixed that I think that that would be a suitable protection system.

222
00:20:46,400 --> 00:20:54,270
Right. What techniques does Code Gate use to ensure that secrets are not accidentally leaked?

223
00:20:54,280 --> 00:20:56,590
So we do several things.

224
00:20:56,590 --> 00:21:05,190
For personal information, we actually use system been in part of machine learning for a while near entity recognition.

225
00:21:05,190 --> 00:21:13,270
So we have a small model which is able to recognize credit card numbers, medical numbers, social security numbers,

226
00:21:13,270 --> 00:21:19,190
personal details, phone numbers, emails, any sort of PII.

227
00:21:19,190 --> 00:21:21,670
This model is able to effectively match quite well.

228
00:21:21,670 --> 00:21:28,990
We also have forms of sensing technology for keys and passwords.

229
00:21:28,990 --> 00:21:38,390
So, we use entropy detection, different systems there to see that something is likely to be a password or a cryptographic token, an API key.

230
00:21:38,390 --> 00:21:44,790
We leverage these machine learning techniques to be able to pattern match effectively sensitive materials.

231
00:21:44,790 --> 00:21:51,070
And then what we do in CodeGate, CodeGate is effectively an inline system.

232
00:21:51,070 --> 00:21:56,630
So, when you converse with an LLM, you're actually using RESTful APIs.

233
00:21:56,630 --> 00:21:58,870
Okay, so it's like any other application.

234
00:21:58,870 --> 00:22:02,070
Your coding assistant is a client.

235
00:22:02,070 --> 00:22:07,350
It will take content, the code, or perhaps if you've typed something into a chat box,

236
00:22:07,350 --> 00:22:16,270
it will take that content and it will load it into a JSON payload which will be posted to a REST API

237
00:22:16,270 --> 00:22:24,150
where the payload will be processed, a response will come back, and it's very much that typical sort of RESTful flow that we're all used to.

238
00:22:24,150 --> 00:22:28,390
And so,

239
00:22:28,390 --> 00:22:38,390
what we are able to do is capture this payload as it comes through CodeGate on its way to the LLM inference point, and then we scrutinize it, we check it for secrets.

240
00:22:38,390 --> 00:22:46,910
On the return path, we check it for malicious packages, and we effectively are able to trace these things on transit, okay?

241
00:22:46,920 --> 00:22:51,590
But, the key thing with CodeGate is this happens locally on your machine.

242
00:22:51,590 --> 00:22:57,430
So, it's not like we're a kind of an EDR endpoint where you're just sending your secrets to someone else.

243
00:22:57,430 --> 00:23:01,790
This is processed right on your machine, and that information never it your machine.

244
00:23:01,800 --> 00:23:09,910
So, if we pick up that there is a cryptographic token within the prompt payload, what we do is we redact it.

245
00:23:09,910 --> 00:23:15,831
So, we swap that token, let's say it's an API token, for a unique string. Okay?

246
00:23:15,840 --> 00:23:20,990
And that payload then goes off to the LLM, which may well refactor the code.

247
00:23:21,000 --> 00:23:22,950
It could move the lines around.

248
00:23:22,950 --> 00:23:25,550
And then it comes back as a response.

249
00:23:25,550 --> 00:23:31,190
As it When it comes back as a response, we receive it,

250
00:23:31,190 --> 00:23:38,510
we match the placeholder, the nominator of where that secret has been redacted with this particular randomly generated string that we have.

251
00:23:38,520 --> 00:23:40,190
And then we swap it back.

252
00:23:40,190 --> 00:23:42,470
We put the secret back in.

253
00:23:42,470 --> 00:23:47,471
So, that when the code appears in your IDE, you can see your API token there. Okay?

254
00:23:47,471 --> 00:23:51,910
And so, what effectively has happened is it's been redacted on the fly.

255
00:23:51,910 --> 00:23:53,870
And we do this with all personal information as well.

256
00:23:53,870 --> 00:24:03,510
If somebody was to accidentally send a file or a file was to be sent to an LLM and it contains a credit card number, we would redact it on the fly.

257
00:24:03,510 --> 00:24:05,710
Out it goes, goes to the LLM.

258
00:24:05,710 --> 00:24:08,670
The LLM does whatever it needs to do, refactor it, fix a bug.

259
00:24:08,680 --> 00:24:10,830
The content comes back.

260
00:24:10,830 --> 00:24:18,710
And then in line, we switch back the redacted string to the cryptographic token or password or credit card number.

261
00:24:18,710 --> 00:24:20,910
And then it appears back in your IDE.

262
00:24:20,910 --> 00:24:29,550
But we also decorate the prompt so that via the code in the system, we'll give a warning and we'll say, "There was a credit card number that was found in your prompt.

263
00:24:29,550 --> 00:24:39,270
We actually protected that for you so you don't have to take any further action, but you should perhaps be aware of why this is a risk and here are some best practices that you can implement."

264
00:24:39,280 --> 00:24:45,190
We're just using standard machine learning technologies here to provide these protections.

265
00:24:45,190 --> 00:24:47,150
We do something similar with malicious packages.

266
00:24:47,150 --> 00:24:55,271
We have a database of malicious packages that we've created over the years in Stack Club. Uh we also run our own SCA system.

267
00:24:55,280 --> 00:25:02,790
We we our own threat hunters that report packages as being malicious or, you know, deprecated, no longer archived.

268
00:25:02,800 --> 00:25:11,030
And if we see the LLM recommend one of these to the coding assistant, what we do is as soon as we see something that looks like a package name,

269
00:25:11,030 --> 00:25:22,670
we have a vectorized database and an embeddings model, and we do a similarity search around that name to see if it matches anything within our vulnerability database.

270
00:25:22,670 --> 00:25:30,030
If it does, then we're able to flag that to the user before they run pip install or npm install and end up backdooring their machine.

271
00:25:30,030 --> 00:25:38,070
So, we do similarity search because sometimes uh there can be typo squatting attacks as well, and that allows us to sense that those are happening.

272
00:25:38,070 --> 00:25:41,790
So, yeah, with CodeGate you you get all of these controls locally in your machine.

273
00:25:41,790 --> 00:25:48,830
You get the ability to redact any personal information or secrets, but your code still comes back refactored.

274
00:25:48,830 --> 00:25:51,470
It doesn't disturb your your flow, effectively.

275
00:25:51,480 --> 00:25:53,830
Your development flow is unhindered.

276
00:25:53,830 --> 00:25:56,030
You don't have to take any action.

277
00:25:56,030 --> 00:26:02,510
We just inform you that we've covered your back and that we redacted the secrets and the tokens.

278
00:26:02,510 --> 00:26:09,510
And And likewise, if the LLM recommends any malicious packages or suspicious packages, we're able to raise alert of that.

279
00:26:09,510 --> 00:26:15,670
And we provide all these protections in line and within the safe confines of your machine. Nothing ever leaves your machine.

280
00:26:15,680 --> 00:26:20,190
I think that's all a very clever way of doing things.

281
00:26:20,190 --> 00:26:25,950
So, redacting or replacing that secret information or personal information with something else, so that the flow of programmer is not impacted.

282
00:26:25,960 --> 00:26:28,551
I think that's very key. That's correct. Yeah, very much.

283
00:26:28,560 --> 00:26:41,750
Because, you know, one of one of the things I found being a security engineer, somebody that's always been focused on developing security technologies that will be adopted by engineers, people want to be productive.

284
00:26:41,760 --> 00:26:51,910
And if security is something that hinders them, blocks them, slows them down, they're much less likely to adopt it.

285
00:26:51,910 --> 00:27:02,150
So, for security to be successful with developers, it's it's got to be as seamless as possible because there's always some new shiny framework or API that's got their attention that's going to make them more productive.

286
00:27:02,150 --> 00:27:09,110
Whereas security you don't want them to have to learn about why they should protect themselves against a particular threat

287
00:27:09,110 --> 00:27:17,910
and then have them go through a a long protracted set of steps to implement the security to then be blocked and to want to turn it off cuz it's frustrating.

288
00:27:17,910 --> 00:27:24,470
It's just the not the right way to get developers to embrace security is to make it a a difficult experience.

289
00:27:24,470 --> 00:27:28,270
If you can make it seamless, they're much more likely to adopt it.

290
00:27:28,270 --> 00:27:35,750
I mean, that's one of the things we found with six store when we were trying to get developers to adopt best practices around signing software.

291
00:27:35,760 --> 00:27:40,790
Up until that point it had been quite a a painful frustrating experience.

292
00:27:40,790 --> 00:27:45,711
People were worried about how to manage the keys. Did they need specialist hardware?

293
00:27:45,720 --> 00:27:47,910
How would they rotate keys?

294
00:27:47,910 --> 00:27:54,990
You know, it was these tools that were quite dated like PGP, GPG and it was just a frustrating experience for developers.

295
00:27:55,000 --> 00:28:04,790
So with six store what we got right was we made it very very easy to sign effectively, you know, it's very very minimal effort required from the developer.

296
00:28:04,790 --> 00:28:16,190
So it almost be seamless and that's why we got the adoption then because I when you're trying to convince developers to take on security, it's like trying to encourage your six-year-old to eat the vegetables.

297
00:28:16,190 --> 00:28:18,190
They don't want to.

298
00:28:18,190 --> 00:28:20,950
They don't really you know, they're not really thinking about their health when they get older.

299
00:28:20,960 --> 00:28:24,390
They're thinking about their immediate needs, you know.

300
00:28:24,390 --> 00:28:28,790
Another good analogy I've used is it's it's like selling insurance.

301
00:28:28,790 --> 00:28:32,950
It's protections against something that could possibly happen in the future but might not.

302
00:28:32,950 --> 00:28:44,390
Uh generally people are going to be more focused on what's going to unlock productivity for me now, you know, what's going to allow me to be more productive and move faster.

303
00:28:44,390 --> 00:28:49,390
And so if security is impinging upon that and restricting that, then it's less likely to get adoption with developers.

304
00:28:49,400 --> 00:28:54,950
We'll move to the next theme which I've already touched on, which is around dependency management.

305
00:28:54,950 --> 00:29:01,950
Can you start off by giving an example of or any story where coding assistants have actually introduced dependency management risks?

306
00:29:01,960 --> 00:29:05,230
And how was it found out?

307
00:29:05,230 --> 00:29:08,710
What was the mechanism or what harm could it have caused?

308
00:29:08,720 --> 00:29:17,110
Yes, so we run a system internally at StackHawk,

309
00:29:17,110 --> 00:29:24,950
which scans packages based on there being certain heuristics that determine that they are they're more likely to be of risk.

310
00:29:24,950 --> 00:29:27,670
So, they require closer scrutiny.

311
00:29:27,670 --> 00:29:35,070
So, we have this system which uh monitors packages from dependencies from Python PyPI,

312
00:29:35,070 --> 00:29:40,430
crates for Rust, npm for JavaScript, Maven for Java, and there's probably a few others go as well.

313
00:29:40,430 --> 00:29:48,630
So, we monitor all of these packages and when we see one with suspicious heuristics, we're able to pull it in and take a closer look.

314
00:29:48,630 --> 00:29:51,230
And we found a good number of malicious packages this way.

315
00:29:51,230 --> 00:30:02,470
One of the ones that our threat researcher found was a believed to be staged by a North Korean threat actor, effectively you know, possibly government-sponsored.

316
00:30:02,480 --> 00:30:12,870
And the package in question was used as part of a series of mock fake interviews with developers.

317
00:30:12,870 --> 00:30:27,750
So, they would be contacted on LinkedIn offered a role with a very high-profile, high-paying company and told, "All you have to do is a coding challenge. Here are the instructions." And the instructions were to download this package that we found.

318
00:30:27,750 --> 00:30:35,630
And once you download it and execute it by running npm install, it would effectively backdoor your machine.

319
00:30:35,630 --> 00:30:44,270
So, it would it was predominantly focused on Macs, but your machine would be backdoored and the attackers would start to exfiltrate information to our Telegram server.

320
00:30:44,270 --> 00:30:47,590
And this was, you know, an attack that we found in the wild.

321
00:30:47,590 --> 00:30:56,990
And one of the things we noticed was that we would go to these very, very, very high-profile cutting-edge, widely used frontier models.

322
00:30:56,990 --> 00:31:05,670
And months later we would say, "Hey, can you tell me how to use this package?" And it would say, "Sure, yeah, this is certainly this is how to use this package." And it would generate a code snippet.

323
00:31:05,670 --> 00:31:12,230
And then it would say, "And then run npm install bad package." And obviously replacing the bad package name here.

324
00:31:12,230 --> 00:31:17,550
And so it was effectively telling people, "Yep, go ahead, use this package.

325
00:31:17,550 --> 00:31:20,790
In fact, here's how to install the package." Which would have resulted in their machines being backdoored.

326
00:31:20,790 --> 00:31:29,510
So that was one of the reasons that we started to look at introducing this system that could protect people against these malicious packages.

327
00:31:29,510 --> 00:31:44,510
Because it's was quite clear that we were asking some of the top coding assistants, some of the top models, "Could we use a bad package?" And it would say, "Certainly you can, and here's how to." So that was something that we saw quite commonly.

328
00:31:44,510 --> 00:31:47,830
Which was obviously a very, very clear security risk.

329
00:31:47,840 --> 00:31:55,870
And I believe the reason for this was the topic you mentioned about training data being stale.

330
00:31:55,870 --> 00:31:59,030
So LLM had not caught on to the fact that this was a malicious package.

331
00:31:59,040 --> 00:32:02,190
Yes, very much.

332
00:32:02,190 --> 00:32:06,510
And also, a lot of the time when these packages, these malicious packages are crafted,

333
00:32:06,510 --> 00:32:14,990
what they will do is they will choose a name that is very close to the name of a popular package.

334
00:32:14,990 --> 00:32:22,350
So for example, there is the the Python package called requests, which a lot of people use as a HTTP client.

335
00:32:22,350 --> 00:32:25,511
It's very, very widely used. Millions of downloads a week.

336
00:32:25,511 --> 00:32:32,311
And you could take the S and you can maybe switch swap it to a Z, requests with a Z. Okay?

337
00:32:32,311 --> 00:32:34,190
That's called a typosquatting attack.

338
00:32:34,190 --> 00:32:39,910
The idea is somebody fumbles on a keyboard, they make a typo, they download and install your package and they're compromised.

339
00:32:39,910 --> 00:32:48,150
Now, the thing is with neural networks, they're a very, very large collection of vectorized weights and biases, okay?

340
00:32:48,150 --> 00:32:53,390
And how they work is they take natural language and they tokenize it.

341
00:32:53,390 --> 00:33:03,430
And then they perform these multiplication matrix multiplication, these various math takes place where they calculate the predicted next token.

342
00:33:03,430 --> 00:33:16,590
You know, there's all sorts of things such as a cosine distance, and they're effectively taking words, breaking them down into these mathematical numbers, and then they're looking for a close proximity patterns within the neural network. That's probably not the right language to use.

343
00:33:16,600 --> 00:33:23,550
If somebody there's a neural network expert, they'll probably be raising their eyebrows, but I'm just trying to make this sort of accessible for all.

344
00:33:23,560 --> 00:33:29,590
They find a very similar close proximity matches.

345
00:33:29,600 --> 00:33:35,310
And then they again decode that to natural language.

346
00:33:35,310 --> 00:33:40,910
So, if you make spelling mistakes with an LLM, it often won't mention them.

347
00:33:40,910 --> 00:33:49,310
It won't pull you up on them because it knows what you're trying to say because it knows of words that are of close proximity to the word that you've used.

348
00:33:49,320 --> 00:33:55,670
And within the wider context, it's able to match similarity.

349
00:33:55,670 --> 00:34:00,590
It's able to sort of find similar calculations that produce a result of a natural language response.

350
00:34:00,600 --> 00:34:04,030
So, you might have the dog sat on the mat, okay?

351
00:34:04,030 --> 00:34:13,310
It's a very similar mathematically to a very sort of very close set of words and letters when tokenized into a neural network.

352
00:34:13,310 --> 00:34:22,590
And so, you can start to see where if you would said to not a neural network or to a large language model, "Uh how can I use package name with slight typo?"

353
00:34:22,600 --> 00:34:31,510
it's going to calculate Calculate probably not being the right word that you are inferring this particular popular package.

354
00:34:31,510 --> 00:34:35,270
And cuz it's such a popular package, it would have appeared a lot within its training data.

355
00:34:35,270 --> 00:34:41,629
So, the weights and biases would naturally allow it to match to the popular package.

356
00:34:41,639 --> 00:34:44,110
Okay.

357
00:34:44,110 --> 00:34:49,389
So, that's a good example of where large language models are prone to not being aware of typosquatting attacks, you know.

358
00:34:49,399 --> 00:34:52,510
There's that one that they do quite often.

359
00:34:52,510 --> 00:34:57,270
They say, "How many Rs in strawberry?" And the LLM is just convinced that it's two.

360
00:34:57,270 --> 00:35:01,710
And you try to convince it it's three, and it just it will not have it.

361
00:35:01,710 --> 00:35:03,670
It's you know, it's to do with this uh vectorization.

362
00:35:03,670 --> 00:35:09,790
They're these huge high-dimensional spaces of these float numbers effectively.

363
00:35:09,790 --> 00:35:15,430
These these vectors and they calculate similar distances around words.

364
00:35:15,430 --> 00:35:17,390
That's how they appear to be human.

365
00:35:17,390 --> 00:35:19,790
So, there's that that's obviously a key risk as well.

366
00:35:19,800 --> 00:35:29,030
On the example that you gave for typosquatting for requests package, won't the LLM naturally pick up the package which is most popular?

367
00:35:29,030 --> 00:35:34,310
Why would it go and pick a package with a typo or a spelling mistake in it?

368
00:35:34,320 --> 00:35:37,030
Well, interestingly, they do.

369
00:35:37,030 --> 00:35:39,510
That's the interesting thing.

370
00:35:39,510 --> 00:35:46,350
When you do give them a word which is has a slight character difference, they still will go with that recommendation.

371
00:35:46,350 --> 00:35:56,951
Go with that recommendation is probably not the right way to articulate speaking about a neural network, but they do go along with the original word that you gave them. I'm not sure why that is.

372
00:35:56,951 --> 00:36:04,350
I mean, you know, these these neural networks, there's a whole study group that are trying to figure out what's going on inside them really cuz you know,

373
00:36:04,350 --> 00:36:10,950
there are aspects of them that we still don't understand really how they operate certain way, but it's just a pattern that they they do follow.

374
00:36:10,960 --> 00:36:21,710
Do the security risks differ between companies using proprietary models like GitHub Copilot versus open-source models like Deep Seek or StarCoder?

