00:36:53,950 --> 00:37:00,110
If you make any sort of trust, it's based on them being an established corporation with a brand to protect, like I spoke about earlier.

384
00:37:00,120 --> 00:37:09,870
Now, open-source models, there are I mean, people call open-source models open-source models, but there is some nuance to this effectively.

385
00:37:09,870 --> 00:37:17,390
Some models are they have an open license, so they have an unrestricted license, like Llama,

386
00:37:17,390 --> 00:37:28,910
for example, which deems people to label them as open-source, but they're not really truly open-source in a way, because the weights are not open or the the data that was used to train the model is is not freely available.

387
00:37:28,920 --> 00:37:39,670
So, for you to be able to really trust the model, whether it's open or closed, I guess by definition of it being closed, this would not be possible, you would need to have reproducibility.

