00:37:39,680 --> 00:37:46,390
You'd need to be able to take the data set, train the model, and then end up with an identical model.

389
00:37:46,390 --> 00:37:51,071
It's a reproducible training flow. The pipeline is reproducible.

390
00:37:51,071 --> 00:38:03,030
And that is really the only way to be able to 100% trust a model is to have that level of provenance, to be able to take all the component parts and end up with the same result in large language model.

391
00:38:03,040 --> 00:38:10,470
So, I guess, can you trust open-source models with there being different variations of open-source models?

392
00:38:10,480 --> 00:38:15,350
Perhaps, because there is a bit more transparency.

393
00:38:15,350 --> 00:38:20,910
That is certainly a a factor, but to truly trust the model, you would need to have the actual training data, as well.

394
00:38:20,910 --> 00:38:29,750
You would need and you would need the computational resources to perform the training, which, let's be honest, very few of us have.

395
00:38:29,750 --> 00:38:36,270
Now, one of the efforts that is looking to solve this is part of the Six Store project, which I started a few years ago.

396
00:38:36,270 --> 00:38:41,950
And there is some work around model provenance, which is just kind of 1.0 release.

397
00:38:41,950 --> 00:38:51,430
And this is being used by various people such as Google and Nvidia and and other entities are starting to either explore or leverage this form of provenance.

398
00:38:51,440 --> 00:38:59,990
And this consists of cryptographic signing of the different flows of a training pipeline.

399
00:38:59,990 --> 00:39:04,790
So, that you can then get guarantees around the integrity and the provenance of the model.

400
00:39:04,790 --> 00:39:17,510
So, there is some good work going in that direction to make these models more accountable and transparent and and for you to have some guarantees around the source of origin, like who did actually train this model?

401
00:39:17,510 --> 00:39:20,950
Is it the person or the people that it claims to originate from?

402
00:39:20,950 --> 00:39:30,270
With a cryptographic provenance, you can get those assurances that it is a model that comes from the entity that it claims to have come from.

403
00:39:30,270 --> 00:39:35,150
So, again, this is same problem, same technology that's just rehashed for a new technology.

404
00:39:35,150 --> 00:39:46,030
We we had this for quite a while with container images and packages, understanding the source of origin, supply chain security, an area that I've I've I've worked in for quite a while myself.

405
00:39:46,030 --> 00:39:50,670
All of these technologies have been repurposed for the AI age.

406
00:39:50,670 --> 00:39:53,470
So, it's It's like any, you know, the old ideas to the new ideas.

407
00:39:53,480 --> 00:39:57,830
All right, and make sure we link to Six Store in our podcast notes.

408
00:39:57,840 --> 00:40:02,750
Sure. Before we wrap up, do you have any thoughts on what's next in AI security?

409
00:40:02,760 --> 00:40:08,150
What are the emerging risks and what are the key mitigating solutions that are being worked on?

410
00:40:08,160 --> 00:40:10,030
That's a very good question.

411
00:40:10,030 --> 00:40:12,110
It's a very good question.

412
00:40:12,110 --> 00:40:17,390
I think a lot of what I've spoken about today are yet to really sort of escalate as being problems.

413
00:40:17,390 --> 00:40:23,270
So, I think a lot of what we've touched upon today are some of the future risks.

414
00:40:23,270 --> 00:40:33,990
But, outside of that, I think, you know, the things that are particularly concerning, and this perhaps goes above security as well, is how influential these things are becoming in our lives, okay?

415
00:40:34,000 --> 00:40:46,510
Personal counselors, accountants, they're becoming deeply ingrained in our lives, large language models, and they appear very trustworthy.

416
00:40:46,510 --> 00:40:49,231
The guide rails are working well. Our models seem safe, okay?

417
00:40:49,231 --> 00:40:56,631
But, these models can experience pretty dramatic malfunctions. They really can.

418
00:40:56,640 --> 00:40:59,390
Catastrophic forgetting, overfitting.

419
00:40:59,400 --> 00:41:06,110
These are things that we've seen play out with frontier widely available models,

420
00:41:06,110 --> 00:41:14,270
but there is that intrinsic capacity for a model to really malfunction on a level that could completely cause mayhem

421
00:41:14,270 --> 00:41:23,990
if other systems are relying on that model for, you know, automation of infrastructure and the services that we rely on to be present every day for society to function.

422
00:41:23,990 --> 00:41:32,470
As LLMs start to become more of a guiding factor in our lives, the safety of LLMs becomes absolutely critical.

423
00:41:32,470 --> 00:41:43,630
And we we spent some time tuning large language models, trying to tune them to be more security-aware, and we experienced them go bad robot, and you know, that it really doesn't take much.

424
00:41:43,630 --> 00:41:51,150
You overfit them to a certain degree, and then suddenly they are just spitting out the same word repeatedly over and over and over and over.

425
00:41:51,150 --> 00:41:53,150
You're just trying to kill the process to stop it.

426
00:41:53,150 --> 00:41:55,710
It's It's effectively gone fully to defectual.

427
00:41:55,710 --> 00:41:58,750
So, I think that is a real concern.

428
00:41:58,750 --> 00:42:01,110
AI is creeping more and more into our lives.

429
00:42:01,120 --> 00:42:02,670
You know, we're starting to rely on it.

430
00:42:02,680 --> 00:42:04,710
You're aware of model context protocol?

431
00:42:04,720 --> 00:42:06,790
Have you come across model context protocol?

432
00:42:06,800 --> 00:42:10,070
No, no, I've not. Yeah, okay.

433
00:42:10,070 --> 00:42:16,470
So, model context protocol is a way of providing an interface for an LLM to control machines.

434
00:42:16,470 --> 00:42:27,590
So, an MCP server effectively becomes a tool that the LLM is aware of that it can use to interface with other machines and systems.

435
00:42:27,590 --> 00:42:42,590
Okay, so people are producing these MCP servers where an LLM can control your Google Drive or it can it can create issues and pull requests within a GitHub repository or or it could connect to a Jira system and create tickets.

436
00:42:42,590 --> 00:42:44,830
And it's it's a very interesting technology is.

437
00:42:44,830 --> 00:42:55,230
You can see a lot of utility in this where LLMs are effectively be able to autonomously interact with systems and automate these processes.

438
00:42:55,230 --> 00:43:02,190
So, they can go beyond just generating code and and engaging with a human, they can start to control machines.

439
00:43:02,190 --> 00:43:11,270
And that's of course a big concern, you know, because if these models were to malfunction or to hallucinate and they have the control of machines,

440
00:43:11,270 --> 00:43:17,110
then you can start to see where, you know, it's it becomes quite obvious where that can be very concerning from a security angle.

441
00:43:17,110 --> 00:43:24,910
And these are technologies that are very much under hype at the moment starting to really get traction, especially model context protocol.

442
00:43:24,910 --> 00:43:32,110
It's one that's lots of MCP servers coming online to allow you to interface and control applications and machines.

443
00:43:32,110 --> 00:43:37,910
These are, you know, they're JavaScript based and not saying that JavaScript's a bad language,

444
00:43:37,910 --> 00:43:45,350
but it's a language that is a little bit easier to potentially foot gun yourself in regards to security, web security and so forth.

445
00:43:45,350 --> 00:43:54,070
Like any language, any language can be dangerous if it's used in the wrong way, but it's, you know, it's an area that's been known for web exploits quite commonly.

446
00:43:54,070 --> 00:43:59,990
You know, it's probably one of the ones that's that's seen more risks manifest than most languages.

447
00:43:59,990 --> 00:44:02,630
I guess because it's web facing.

448
00:44:02,630 --> 00:44:15,150
And so, there's a lot to be concerned around with with this ability for large language models to control machines and access APIs and move data around and and then of course you have agents.

449
00:44:15,150 --> 00:44:25,310
So agents being these autonomous goal-driven entities that are connected to a large language model and they are able to go off and with the goal of solving a task.

450
00:44:25,310 --> 00:44:38,190
Effectively, you know, if one of these was to become misaligned or mispurposed, it could feed false information, it could perform damaging integrations with other systems. There's a lot that could go wrong.

451
00:44:38,190 --> 00:44:39,990
There's a lot that could go wrong.

452
00:44:39,990 --> 00:44:46,470
So yes, very much a brave new world that we're entering and I don't think life is going to get any quieter for us security folks for quite a while.

453
00:44:46,480 --> 00:44:48,390
Absolutely.

